# Shell scripts run on the Linux CI runner — CRLF would break the shebang
# ("bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M"). Keep them LF regardless of core.autocrlf.
*.sh text eol=lf
# drizzle-kit always emits LF; the committed migration must not flip to CRLF
# on a Windows checkout, or infra/src/schema-generated.test.ts's comparison
# would be diffing against a file git itself considers unchanged.
*.sql text eol=lf
# Markdown blobs are LF and a Windows checkout must not flip them: an edit that
# detects the line ending from the working copy then commits CRLF, and git skips
# CRLF cleaning for a path whose blob already has it, so the damage is sticky.
# One branch lost a five-line repair inside a 1,190-line phantom diff that way.
*.md text eol=lf

# Machine-written JSON. With `core.autocrlf=true` and no attribute, CHECKOUT
# gives the working copy CRLF while the blob stays LF; a regenerator then
# rewrites the file LF, and the pair reads as MODIFIED with an empty textual
# diff until git next touches it. Noise after every run, and on 2026-08-28 it
# blocked a rebase mid-merge. A pattern rather than two names, so the next
# machine-written JSON does not silently miss it.
*.json text eol=lf
